1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,469
On Mars, the sunset is blue.

2
00:00:02,509 --> 00:00:04,623
This is not an artist's guess.

3
00:00:04,663 --> 00:00:07,022
Rovers have photographed it.

4
00:00:07,750 --> 00:00:11,427
The same Sun sets on every world in the
solar system.

5
00:00:11,467 --> 00:00:13,531
But no two sunsets look alike.

6
00:00:13,571 --> 00:00:17,358
Here they are, one by one, through the
same lens.

7
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:22,246
Start with the one we know.

8
00:00:22,286 --> 00:00:25,025
From Earth, the Sun is half a degree wide,

9
00:00:25,065 --> 00:00:28,987
and takes about two minutes to slip below
the horizon.

10
00:00:29,750 --> 00:00:33,383
Air molecules scatter blue light far more
than red.

11
00:00:33,423 --> 00:00:37,272
At noon, that scattered blue fills the
sky. At sunset,

12
00:00:37,312 --> 00:00:42,961
the light crosses so much air that most of
the blue is scattered away before it

13
00:00:43,001 --> 00:00:45,728
reaches you. What is left is red.

14
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:48,823
Mars.

15
00:00:48,863 --> 00:00:54,128
Half as far again from the Sun, so the
disc is about two thirds the size.

16
00:00:54,168 --> 00:00:59,505
The air is thin, less than a hundredth of
Earth's, but it is full of fine,

17
00:00:59,545 --> 00:01:00,886
rust-coloured dust.

18
00:01:00,926 --> 00:01:04,546
By day, that dust turns the sky
butterscotch.

19
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:10,893
At sunset, the grains are just the right
size to scatter blue light forward,

20
00:01:10,933 --> 00:01:13,110
in a tight cone around the Sun.

21
00:01:13,150 --> 00:01:15,111
So the colours are reversed:

22
00:01:15,151 --> 00:01:17,932
a tawny sky, and a cold blue glow.

23
00:01:20,750 --> 00:01:22,616
Mercury has almost no air.

24
00:01:22,656 --> 00:01:24,743
So there is no colour at all.

25
00:01:24,783 --> 00:01:30,535
The sky is black, even at noon, and the
Sun, up to three times wider than ours,

26
00:01:30,575 --> 00:01:34,445
drops behind the rock with nothing to
soften it.

27
00:01:35,250 --> 00:01:36,587
It takes its time.

28
00:01:36,627 --> 00:01:40,873
One day here lasts a hundred and seventy-
six Earth days.

29
00:01:40,913 --> 00:01:44,011
And near its closest approach to the Sun,

30
00:01:44,051 --> 00:01:47,837
Mercury's orbital motion briefly outruns
its spin.

31
00:01:47,877 --> 00:01:54,349
From some places, the Sun sets, stops,
rises again, and then sets a second time.

32
00:01:57,250 --> 00:02:00,637
Venus has the opposite problem. Too much
air.

33
00:02:00,677 --> 00:02:05,588
From the surface, under a permanent deck
of sulphuric acid cloud,

34
00:02:05,628 --> 00:02:08,330
you never see the Sun's disc at all.

35
00:02:08,370 --> 00:02:13,509
Daylight is a dim orange overcast, and
sunset is just that overcast,

36
00:02:13,549 --> 00:02:15,270
slowly going dark.

37
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:16,811
Very slowly.

38
00:02:16,851 --> 00:02:21,280
Venus spins backwards, and one turn takes
longer than its year.

39
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:26,032
From one sunrise to the next is a hundred
and seventeen Earth days.

40
00:02:26,072 --> 00:02:30,394
And if you could see the Sun, it would
rise in the west.

41
00:02:33,250 --> 00:02:36,549
The giant planets have no ground to stand
on.

42
00:02:36,589 --> 00:02:39,368
So float at the cloud tops of Jupiter.

43
00:02:39,408 --> 00:02:43,597
Out here, the Sun is a fifth as wide as it
is from Earth,

44
00:02:43,637 --> 00:02:45,897
and twenty-seven times fainter.

45
00:02:45,937 --> 00:02:49,013
But Jupiter spins once in under ten hours.

46
00:02:49,053 --> 00:02:54,225
So that tiny disc drops through the
horizon in about ten seconds.

47
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,718
At Saturn, the Sun is a tenth the width.

48
00:02:59,758 --> 00:03:02,890
But it is not the Sun you would be looking
at.

49
00:03:02,930 --> 00:03:07,096
The rings rise out of the haze and arch
across the whole sky:

50
00:03:07,136 --> 00:03:10,864
countless pieces of ice, catching the last
light.

51
00:03:13,750 --> 00:03:18,122
Uranus lies on its side, and one orbit
takes eighty-four years.

52
00:03:18,162 --> 00:03:23,795
Near a pole, the Sun circles the sky without
setting for about forty-two of them,

53
00:03:23,835 --> 00:03:26,107
spiralling lower with every turn.

54
00:03:26,147 --> 00:03:31,920
When it finally touches the horizon, the
tiny disc is gone within a couple of days.

55
00:03:31,960 --> 00:03:34,581
But the twilight can linger for years.

56
00:03:34,621 --> 00:03:40,014
Farther out still, at Neptune, the Sun
shrinks to a thirtieth the width.

57
00:03:42,750 --> 00:03:47,148
Titan, Saturn's largest moon, has air
thicker than Earth's,

58
00:03:47,188 --> 00:03:49,781
and an orange haze of organic smog.

59
00:03:49,821 --> 00:03:53,994
In two thousand and five, the Huygens
probe landed here,

60
00:03:54,034 --> 00:03:56,025
among pebbles of water ice,

61
00:03:56,065 --> 00:04:00,703
in light about a thousand times dimmer
than an Earth day.

62
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:05,346
The haze lets red light through, and
blocks the blue.

63
00:04:05,386 --> 00:04:10,184
So over a sea of liquid methane, the Sun
never shows a clean edge.

64
00:04:10,224 --> 00:04:12,530
It sinks into a dull amber glow.

65
00:04:12,570 --> 00:04:18,052
And the dusk drags on, because one day on
Titan lasts sixteen of ours.

66
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:24,308
And Pluto. Forty times farther out than
Earth.

67
00:04:24,348 --> 00:04:28,530
The Sun is no longer a disc. It is a star:
a single point,

68
00:04:28,570 --> 00:04:34,161
yet still about two hundred and fifty
times brighter than our full Moon.

69
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:40,032
Noon on Pluto is about as bright as Earth
a few minutes after sunset.

70
00:04:40,072 --> 00:04:42,532
NASA calls that moment Pluto time.

71
00:04:42,572 --> 00:04:48,876
And when New Horizons looked back, it saw
layer upon layer of haze, glowing blue.

72
00:04:51,750 --> 00:04:54,497
Beyond our own Sun, it gets stranger.

73
00:04:54,537 --> 00:04:58,112
Kepler-16b circles two stars. It is a gas
giant.

74
00:04:58,152 --> 00:05:04,137
But from a moon of it, if it has one, you
would watch two suns go down together:

75
00:05:04,177 --> 00:05:06,787
one orange, one small and red.

76
00:05:09,750 --> 00:05:14,260
Every one of these views is a
reconstruction, built from measurements.

77
00:05:14,300 --> 00:05:16,794
Robots have photographed a few of them.

78
00:05:16,834 --> 00:05:19,979
No person has ever stood under any of
them.

79
00:05:21,250 --> 00:05:26,013
There is only one sunset that human eyes
have watched from the ground.

80
00:05:26,053 --> 00:05:28,414
This one. It happens every evening.

81
00:05:28,454 --> 00:05:30,807
It is worth looking up for.
